MVM.registerModule({
  "id": "m03",
  "summary": "Carry out a routine service and a systematic inspection: follow a schedule, check fluids, change oil and filters, inspect tyres, belts, lights and brakes, road test, and hand the vehicle back with a clear record.",
  "outcomes": ["Use a service schedule and job card, and record findings with a simple traffic-light rating.","Identify the main components under the bonnet and check each fluid level correctly.","Change engine oil and filters safely, fit the sump plug correctly and dispose of waste properly.","Read tyre markings, check tread depth and pressure, and rotate and refit wheels to the correct torque.","Inspect belts, hoses, lights, wipers, the battery and the visible brake and steering parts.","Road test a vehicle safely and explain the findings and next service date to the customer."],
  "cheatsheet": ["Follow the service schedule in the manufacturer's manual, and shorten the interval for severe use.","Inspect in a fixed order and record each item as green, amber or red.","Check engine oil with the vehicle level and the engine off for a few minutes; the level should lie between the marks.","Warm engine, drain oil into a container, fit a new sump washer, torque the plug, then refill to the correct quantity.","Used oil, filters and coolant are hazardous waste; never pour them into drains or onto the ground.","Tyre size 205/55 R16: 205 mm wide, sidewall 55% of width, radial, 16 inch rim.","Check tyre pressure when cold, using the pressure on the vehicle placard rather than the tyre sidewall.","Tighten wheel nuts in a crisscross order to the specified torque, and recheck after about 50 km.","A road test comes after the work is finished and checked, and it must be done safely and legally."],
  "lessons": [
    {"id": "m03-l1", "title": "Service schedules and inspection", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Routine servicing keeps a vehicle safe, reliable and cheaper to run. A technician works to a **service schedule** and records what was done on a **job card**. This lesson explains how to plan a service and how to inspect a vehicle in a way that does not miss anything."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Service schedules"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A service schedule is a list of tasks and the distance or time at which each one is due. The manufacturer publishes it in the owner's handbook and the workshop manual. Typical tasks are an oil and filter change, air and fuel filter replacement, spark plug replacement, brake fluid renewal and a coolant check. Intervals are given by distance (for example every 10,000 km) or by time (for example every 12 months), whichever comes first. Check the manufacturer's workshop manual for the intervals for the vehicle you are working on."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Types of service"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Workshops usually offer a few levels of service, and each customer needs the one that suits their vehicle and use."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Service","Typical content","Typical timing"],"rows":[["Interim or basic","Oil and filter, fluid levels, tyre check, lights, a short inspection","Every 5,000 to 10,000 km or six months"],["Full or annual","Interim items plus air filter, brake check and fluid, plugs where due, a detailed inspection","Every 12 months or 15,000 to 20,000 km"],["Major","Full service plus items due at long intervals, such as timing belt, coolant, gearbox oil, spark plugs","At the distances set in the schedule"],["Condition-based","Work decided by the vehicle's service indicator or measured fluid condition","As the vehicle indicates"]],"caption":"Names and intervals differ between makers and countries. Follow the schedule for the vehicle."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Severe use"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Some operating conditions wear a vehicle faster than normal. These are called **severe use**. Examples are dusty roads, extreme heat, stop-start city traffic, many short trips, towing, carrying heavy loads and poor fuel quality. A vehicle used this way needs shorter intervals, especially for engine oil, air filters and brake fluid. Ask the customer how the vehicle is used and write it on the job card."},
      {"t":"h","text":"The job card"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A job card is a written record of the work. It should show the customer's details, the vehicle registration and VIN, the odometer reading, the work requested, the faults found, the parts and fluids used, the technician's name and the date. It protects you and the customer, and the next technician will rely on it."},
      {"t":"h","text":"A systematic inspection"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Always inspect in the same order, so nothing is missed. A common order is: outside the vehicle, inside the cabin, under the bonnet, under the vehicle, then the road test. Write down each finding as you go. Do not rely on memory."},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Green:** the item is in good condition and needs no action.","**Amber:** the item is wearing and will need attention soon. Tell the customer.","**Red:** the item is unsafe or has failed. It needs attention now."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Warning lights worth knowing"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Light","Colour","Meaning and action"],"rows":[["Oil pressure","Red","Low oil pressure. Stop the engine at once and check the level."],["Coolant temperature","Red","Engine overheating. Stop safely and let it cool."],["Battery or charging","Red","The charging system has failed. The battery will run flat."],["Brake system","Red","Low brake fluid, handbrake on, or a hydraulic fault. Do not drive until checked."],["Engine management","Amber","An emission or engine control fault. Read the codes soon."],["ABS","Amber","Anti-lock braking is off. The normal brakes still work."],["Airbag","Red or amber","The restraint system has a fault. Do not work on it without the correct procedure."]],"caption":"Red usually means stop now or soon. Amber means get it checked."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Using a written checklist"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A checklist makes the inspection the same every time. It lists every item with a tick box, or a green, amber or red mark, and a space for comments such as the pad thickness in millimetres. Complete it as you go and give the customer a copy. A good checklist reduces mistakes, protects you if a fault was missed or ignored, and helps you sell necessary work honestly."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Before you start, ask the customer if they have noticed any noises, smells, vibrations, warning lights or changes in how the vehicle drives. Their answers often point directly to a fault."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Inspection is not repair. Record what you find and agree the work with the customer before you carry out anything that was not on the original job card."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m03-l2", "title": "Under the bonnet and fluid checks", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"The engine bay holds the fluids and components that need regular checks. This lesson shows what you will find there and how to check each fluid."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"underbonnet-layout"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Know the layout"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Open the **bonnet (hood)** and make sure the stay or prop holds it securely. The layout differs between vehicles, but you will usually find the engine, the battery, the air filter box, the radiator and coolant reservoir, the brake fluid reservoir, the washer bottle, the power-steering reservoir (on hydraulic systems) and the fuse box. Learn where each is before you start a service."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Engine oil"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Park the vehicle on level ground. Switch off the engine and wait a few minutes so the oil drains back into the sump. Pull the **dipstick**, wipe it clean, push it fully home and pull it out again. The level should lie between the minimum and maximum marks. Oil that is black and gritty, or milky, needs to be investigated. Milky oil can mean coolant is leaking into it."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Reading the condition of the oil"},
      {"t":"table","head":["What you see","What it may mean"],"rows":[["Honey-brown, clean","Fresh or lightly used oil"],["Dark and thick","Used oil. It may be due for a change. Diesel engines darken oil quickly, which is normal."],["Milky or creamy","Coolant or moisture in the oil, perhaps a head gasket leak, a cracked head or condensation from short trips"],["Smells of fuel and feels thin","Fuel dilution from short trips, a leaking injector or a rich mixture"],["Gritty or metal flakes","Wear or dirt. Investigate the cause before refilling."],["Level falling steadily","Burning oil, a leak, or worn rings and valve seals"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Oil grades in plain terms"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Oil is described by its **viscosity grade**, for example 5W-30. The first number with the W (for winter) shows how well the oil flows when cold. A lower number flows more easily at low temperature. The second number shows how thick the oil is at running temperature. Use the grade and specification in the workshop manual. Using the wrong oil can harm the engine."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Coolant, brake fluid and others"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Coolant:** check the level in the expansion tank only when the engine is cold. Never open a radiator cap on a hot engine, because the system is under pressure and can scald you.","**Brake fluid:** the level should lie between the marks. A low level can mean worn brake pads or a leak. Brake fluid absorbs water and must be renewed at the interval in the schedule.","**Power-steering fluid:** check the level on the reservoir marks. A whining noise on turning can mean the fluid is low.","**Washer fluid:** top up with screen wash, not plain water in cold climates where it may freeze."]},
      {"t":"table","head":["Fluid","When to check","Warning sign"],"rows":[["Engine oil","Engine off, level ground","Milky or very low"],["Coolant","Engine cold only","Level dropping regularly"],["Brake fluid","Any time, at the reservoir","Falling level, dark colour"],["Power-steering fluid","Any time","Noise on turning, foamy fluid"]],"caption":"Basic fluid checks"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Checking transmission and other fluids"},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Automatic transmission fluid** is checked on many vehicles with a dipstick, with the engine running and at the temperature the manual gives. Some modern gearboxes are sealed and have no dipstick, and are checked through a plug with a scan tool. **Manual gearbox oil** is normally checked at the filler plug on the side of the gearbox. The level should reach the bottom of the hole. **Differential oil** is checked in the same way. A final-drive leak shows as a wet area at the axle seals."},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Hydraulic clutch fluid** is usually shared with the brake reservoir. If the clutch pedal sinks or feels soft, look for a leak at the master or slave cylinder before you top up."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Topping up and changing"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Topping up adds a small amount of the same fluid to restore the level. Changing replaces all of the old fluid because it has worn out or become contaminated. A fluid that keeps going down has a leak, and topping up is not a cure. Find the leak."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Checking for leaks"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A leak is a fault, however small. Place a clean sheet of card under the vehicle overnight, or look at the underside with the vehicle raised. The colour and position of a drip help to identify the fluid and the source."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Colour and feel","Likely fluid","Common sources"],"rows":[["Dark brown or black, oily","Engine oil","Sump gasket, drain plug, rocker cover, rear main seal"],["Red or pink, oily","Automatic transmission or power steering fluid","Cooler lines, pan gasket, seals, steering rack"],["Green, orange or pink, watery and sweet","Coolant","Hoses, radiator, water pump, heater core"],["Clear or yellowish, slippery","Brake or clutch fluid","Calipers, wheel cylinders, pipes, master cylinder"],["Clear and watery, no smell","Air conditioning condensation","Normal on a hot day. Not a leak."],["Smells of petrol","Fuel","Lines, injectors, tank. A fire risk."]],"caption":"A leak of fuel or brake fluid must be fixed before the vehicle is used."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never mix different types of coolant or brake fluid. Using the wrong type can damage seals or reduce the protection against boiling and corrosion."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Photograph or note each fluid level before you top up. It shows the customer what you found and tells you how fast a fluid is being lost."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m03-l3", "title": "Lubricants, coolants and fluids in depth", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Every fluid in a vehicle has a specific job and a specific specification. Using the wrong one is one of the commonest causes of damage after a service. This lesson explains how engine oil, gear oil, grease, coolant and brake fluid are classified, so you can read a label and choose correctly."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"fluid-condition"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Engine oil"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Engine oil is a **base oil** with additives. The base oil is mineral (refined from crude oil), semi-synthetic (a blend) or fully synthetic (made by chemical processes). Synthetic oils flow better when cold, resist heat and last longer, but cost more. Additives control foaming, wear, rust, deposits, acid and oil thinning."},
      {"t":"p","text":"Two labels matter. The **viscosity grade** from the SAE, such as 5W-30 or 0W-20, shows how thick the oil is cold and hot. The **performance specification** shows how well the oil protects. Common systems are the API (petrol categories start with S and diesel categories with C) and the ACEA (A and B classes for petrol and diesel, and C classes for low-ash oils that protect catalytic converters and particulate filters). The manual lists the viscosity and the specification you need, and the maker's own approvals for some engines."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Viscosity grade","What it means","Typical use"],"rows":[["0W-20, 5W-20","Very thin when cold and hot","Modern fuel-saving engines that call for it"],["5W-30, 0W-30","Thin when cold, medium when hot","Many modern petrol and diesel engines"],["10W-40","Thicker when cold and hot","Older engines and hotter climates, where the manual allows"],["15W-40, 20W-50","Thick","Older or heavy-duty engines, hot climates"]],"caption":"Only use a grade the manual approves. A thicker oil is not always better and can starve tight engines on a cold start."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Gear oil, transmission fluid and grease"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Gear oil** for manual gearboxes and differentials is graded by SAE number (such as 75W-90) and by API GL class. A GL-4 oil is suitable for gearboxes with brass synchroniser rings. A GL-5 oil has strong extreme-pressure additives for hypoid axles, and can attack brass in some gearboxes. Use what the manual specifies.","**Automatic transmission fluid** (ATF) is a hydraulic fluid and a friction-controlling fluid. Specifications differ widely, and the wrong one causes slipping and shuddering. Some gearboxes use a special oil that is not ATF.","**Grease** is oil thickened with soap. A common type is lithium-based, graded by stiffness (an NLGI number such as 2). Constant-velocity joints use special grease with molybdenum. Brake slides need a high-temperature brake grease that does not melt or wash out."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Coolant"},
      {"t":"p","text":"**Coolant** is a mixture of water and antifreeze, usually ethylene glycol, with additives that stop corrosion. Antifreeze lowers the freezing point and raises the boiling point. A 50% mixture protects against freezing to about −37 °C and boils higher than plain water, especially under the pressure of the cooling system. The additives are not all alike. The older **IAT** type is based on silicates and phosphates and is often green. **OAT** and **HOAT** types use organic acids, in other colours. Colour is not a safe guide, so do not mix coolants without checking compatibility, and always use the type in the manual."},
      {"t":"p","text":"Mix concentrate with **distilled or deionised water**, not tap water. Minerals in hard water leave scale and cause corrosion. Replace coolant at the interval in the schedule, often every two to five years, because the additives wear out even if the coolant still looks clean."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Brake fluid"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Brake fluid is a hydraulic fluid that must not boil. Most grades are glycol-based and are given **DOT** numbers that reflect their boiling points. Fluid absorbs water from the air over time, and the wet boiling point is much lower than the dry value."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Grade","Dry boiling point","Wet boiling point","Notes"],"rows":[["DOT 3","At least 205 °C","At least 140 °C","Glycol-based. Common in older vehicles."],["DOT 4","At least 230 °C","At least 155 °C","Glycol-based. Common today."],["DOT 5.1","At least 260 °C","At least 180 °C","Glycol-based. High performance."],["DOT 5","At least 260 °C","At least 180 °C","Silicone-based. Never mix with glycol grades."]],"caption":"Boiling points shown are the standards' minimum values. Use the grade shown on the reservoir cap or in the manual."},
      {"t":"p","text":"Brake fluid can absorb 2 to 3% water within about two years in a vehicle. Test the fluid with a moisture or boiling-point tester, and replace it at the interval in the schedule, normally every two years."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Always use the viscosity, the specification and the type the manufacturer states. If you cannot find the specification, find out before you fill. Do not guess."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never mix DOT 5 silicone brake fluid with glycol brake fluid. Never reuse brake fluid that has been drained. Keep brake fluid away from paintwork and from your eyes."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m03-l4", "title": "Oil and filter service", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"An engine oil and filter change is the most common service job. Done well, it protects the engine. Done badly, it can destroy it. This lesson covers the steps and the common mistakes."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Preparation"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Collect the correct oil, a new oil filter, a new sump plug washer and a drain container large enough for the whole oil quantity. Warm the engine for a few minutes so the oil flows well, then switch it off. Warm oil drains more completely but can burn, so take care. Raise the vehicle safely on a lift or axle stands, as you learned in module 1."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Oil filter types"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **spin-on filter** is a sealed metal canister that screws onto the engine and is replaced as a whole. A **cartridge filter** is a paper element that sits inside a reusable housing. The housing cap holds a sealing ring that must be replaced with the element. Many engines now use cartridge filters, and their caps need a specific torque. Fitting the wrong filter, or one with the wrong bypass valve or anti-drain valve, can starve the engine of oil."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Draining and filter change"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Place the drain container under the sump plug.","Remove the sump plug and let the oil drain fully.","Clean the plug and fit a new washer or seal.","Remove the old oil filter. Check that the old rubber seal came off with it, because a second seal on the housing causes a major leak.","Smear a thin film of clean oil on the new filter's seal and fit it as the maker directs, usually hand tight plus a part turn.","Refit the sump plug and tighten it to the torque in the manual. A cross-threaded or over-tightened plug can strip the sump thread."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Refilling"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Pour in the correct quantity and grade of oil through the filler. The workshop manual gives the capacity. Start with slightly less than the full amount, run the engine for a short time, switch it off, wait, and check the dipstick. Add oil until the level is between the marks. Never overfill, because excess oil can foam and damage the engine."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Finding how much oil to add","steps":["The capacity with filter is 4.3 L. The engine drained about 4.0 L, and the new filter holds 0.3 L when full.","Add about 4.0 L first. Run the engine for a minute so oil fills the filter and the galleries, then stop it and wait about five minutes.","Check the dipstick. If it reads 0.5 L below the maximum mark, add 0.3 L, wait and check again, until it is close to the maximum mark.","Never go above the maximum mark. Too much oil can be churned into foam by the crankshaft and lose its lubricating ability."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Special requirements"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Diesel engines with a particulate filter** often need low-ash oil, as the manual specifies. The wrong oil can block the filter.","**Engines with turbochargers** need good-quality oil and a short idle after hard driving, so the bearings cool.","**Some engines** use an oil level sensor and have no dipstick. Check the level with a scan tool or the instrument display, following the manual.","**Hybrid vehicles** may run the engine rarely, so oil changes may be due by time rather than distance."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"After the job"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Run the engine and check for leaks at the drain plug and filter.","Reset the service indicator or maintenance light as described in the manual.","Note the date, odometer reading and the oil type on the job card and sticker.","Pour used oil into a labelled container and take it to an approved collection point."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Used engine oil is a hazardous waste. It can irritate the skin and pollute water. Wear gloves, wipe spills at once and never pour it down a drain or onto the ground."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Lay out the new filter, washer, oil and torque wrench before you start. Fewer trips mean fewer drips and a quicker job."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Other filters"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The **air filter** stops dust reaching the engine. A blocked filter reduces power and increases fuel use, and it clogs faster in dusty conditions. The **fuel filter** protects the injectors and pump from dirt. The **cabin filter** cleans the air entering the passenger compartment, and a blocked one reduces airflow from the vents. Replace each at the interval in the schedule, or sooner in severe use."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Resetting the service indicator"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Most vehicles have a service reminder. It is reset by a button sequence, the instrument menu or a scan tool. Do this only after the service is done, and never to hide a due service. A vehicle that shows a reminder you did not clear may have an old service record that did not match the actual work."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m03-l5", "title": "Filters, spark plugs, coolant, brake fluid and transmission oil", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Beyond the engine oil, a full service covers items that are changed at longer intervals. This lesson describes each one, why it matters and how to do the job correctly. Always follow the vehicle's workshop manual for intervals, capacities and torque figures."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Air filter"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The air filter stops dust and grit entering the engine. Remove the filter box lid, take out the paper element and hold it to a light. If you cannot see light through it, it is blocked. Replace it at the interval, or sooner in dusty places. Never wash or oil a paper element, and clean the inside of the box before you refit it. A dirty or loose filter box lets unfiltered air through and wears the engine."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Fuel filter"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A fuel filter catches dirt and, in diesel systems, water. A blocked filter starves the engine and loses power. Before you remove a petrol fuel filter, relieve the pressure in the fuel system as the manual says, and catch the fuel. Diesel systems often have a **water separator** with a drain valve, which should be drained at the intervals in the schedule. Replace filters with the arrows facing in the direction of flow."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Spark plugs (petrol engines)"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Spark plugs create the spark that ignites the mixture. Their electrodes wear, so the gap grows and the spark weakens. Copper plugs usually last 20,000 to 30,000 km, and platinum or iridium plugs often 60,000 to 100,000 km, but follow the schedule."},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Let the engine cool, because aluminium heads can be damaged if you remove plugs from a hot engine.","Remove the ignition coil or lead, taking note of its position, and blow out or vacuum any dirt around each plug before you unscrew it.","Unscrew with the correct plug socket and keep the socket straight. Look at the old plug, because its colour shows how the engine is burning: light grey-brown is normal, black and sooty suggests a rich mixture, and white or blistered suggests overheating.","Check the new plug's gap with a gauge and its type against the manual. Do not bend the centre electrode.","Start the thread by hand to avoid cross-threading, and then tighten to the specified torque. Over-tightening can crack the plug or strip the head.","Refit the coils and leads in the right order and check the engine runs smoothly."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Coolant change"},
      {"t":"ol","items":["Work only on a cold engine. Open the cap slowly. Set the heater controls to hot so the heater core drains as well.","Place a container under the radiator and open the drain, collecting the old coolant. Drain the engine block if there is a plug.","Flush the system with clean water if the manual or the condition of the coolant calls for it.","Close the drains and refill with the correct mixture of coolant and distilled water.","Bleed the air: open the bleed screws where fitted, run the engine with the heater on, and top up the level as air escapes. Squeeze hoses gently to help trapped air out.","Check the level again when the engine is cold, and pressure-test the system for leaks.","Store the old coolant in a labelled container for collection. It is poisonous."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Brake fluid change"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Brake fluid is changed by flushing the old fluid out of the system and refilling it with new fluid. The master cylinder reservoir must never run dry, because air entering the system means a full bleed. Start with the wheel furthest from the master cylinder, unless the manual says otherwise, and work toward the nearest. Use a pressure bleeder, a vacuum bleeder or two people. Continue until clean, fresh-looking fluid with no bubbles flows out. Top up the reservoir, test the pedal firmly, and check the brake warning lights."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Manual gearbox and differential oil"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Gear oil is checked and changed at the plugs on the gearbox and differential. Clean the area first. Open the **fill plug** before the drain plug, so that you know you can refill it. Drain the old oil, refit the drain plug with a new washer, and pump or pour in new oil until it just starts to run out of the fill hole, with the vehicle level. Refit the plug and torque it. Check that the oil type is correct for the gearbox. Some gearboxes are filled for life, and the manual says so."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Automatic transmission service"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The procedure depends on the gearbox. Some have a drain plug and a pan with a filter that is removed and cleaned or replaced. Others need a **fluid exchange** machine. Many modern units are sealed and are filled and level-checked through a plug at a set temperature, using a scan tool to read the fluid temperature. Use only the fluid the manual specifies. A wrong fluid, or the wrong level, damages the clutches. If you do not have the correct equipment and information, leave the job to a specialist."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Write the fluid type, the amount you added, the date and the odometer reading on the job card and the service sticker. Everyone will then know exactly what is in the vehicle."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never open a hot cooling system, and never loosen fuel lines without relieving the pressure and keeping sparks away. Never drain brake fluid onto the ground or reuse it."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m03-l6", "title": "Tyres and wheels", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Tyres are the only contact between the vehicle and the road. Checking them is part of every service. This lesson explains how to read a tyre, check its condition and refit wheels safely."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"tyre-wear-patterns"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Reading the sidewall"},
      {"t":"p","text":"The sidewall carries the tyre size and ratings. In 205/55 R16 91V, the 205 is the tyre width in millimetres. The 55 is the **aspect ratio**, meaning the sidewall height is 55% of the width. The R means radial construction. The 16 is the rim diameter in inches. The 91 is the **load index** and the V is the **speed rating**. Use the sizes and ratings given in the vehicle's manual."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"tyre-markings"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Load index, speed rating and tyre age"},
      {"t":"table","head":["Load index","Maximum load per tyre","Speed rating","Maximum speed"],"rows":[["82","475 kg","T","190 km/h"],["88","560 kg","H","210 km/h"],["91","615 kg","V","240 km/h"],["95","690 kg","W","270 km/h"]],"caption":"Use the values the vehicle maker gives. A tyre must never have a lower load index or speed rating than the vehicle requires."},
      {"t":"p","text":"The **DOT code** at the end of a tyre's identification gives the week and year of manufacture. A code of 3523 means week 35 of 2023. Tyres age even when they are unused, because the rubber hardens and cracks. Many makers advise inspection after five years and replacement at about ten years, whatever the tread depth. Check the age of the spare wheel as well."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Tread depth and wear"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Tread channels water away from under the tyre. Many countries set a legal minimum tread depth, often 1.6 mm across the main tread, but a safer minimum for wet roads is around 3 mm. Check your local rule. Look at the wear pattern, because it points to the cause."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Wear pattern","Likely cause"],"rows":[["Both edges worn","Under-inflation"],["Centre worn","Over-inflation"],["One edge worn","Wheel alignment out of specification"],["Patchy or cupped wear","Worn suspension or an unbalanced wheel"]],"caption":"What wear patterns tell you"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Pressure"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Check the pressure when the tyres are cold, because driving heats the air and raises the reading. Use the pressure on the vehicle's placard (inside the door frame or fuel flap) or in the manual, not the maximum pressure moulded on the tyre. Typical car pressures are around 2.0 to 2.5 bar (30 to 36 psi), but always check the manual. Do not forget the spare wheel."},
      {"t":"p","text":"Many vehicles have a **tyre pressure monitoring system** (TPMS). A sensor in each wheel, or the ABS speed sensors, reports a pressure drop and lights a warning. Sensors have batteries that wear out, and they can be damaged when tyres are changed. After changing tyres, the system may need a reset or relearn. A TPMS warning is a reason to check all four tyres with a gauge. It is not a substitute for regular checks."},
      {"t":"p","text":"Under-inflation heats the tyre and can cause sudden failure. Over-inflation reduces grip and wears the centre of the tread. A pressure that falls 0.1 bar (1.5 psi) for every 10 °C drop in temperature explains why tyres need topping up in cold weather."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Damage and repairs"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Look for cuts, bulges, cracks, nails and exposed cord. A bulge in the sidewall means internal damage, and the tyre must be replaced. A puncture in the central tread area can often be repaired properly from the inside. A sidewall or shoulder cannot be repaired."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Rotation and refitting"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Rotating tyres evens out wear. The pattern depends on whether the vehicle is front-, rear- or four-wheel drive and whether the tyres are directional. Refit the wheel, run the nuts on by hand, lower the vehicle and tighten the nuts in a crisscross order to the torque in the manual. Recheck after about 50 km."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Balancing and wheel care"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A wheel and tyre are rarely perfectly balanced. A **wheel balancer** spins the wheel, finds the heavy spot and shows where to fit small **balance weights**. An unbalanced wheel causes vibration at certain speeds, usually felt in the steering wheel for the front and the seat for the rear, and it wears the suspension and tyres."},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Clean the wheel and remove stones from the tread and all old weights before you balance.","Check the rim for bends and the tyre for damage and uneven wear before you spend time balancing.","Fit new valve stems or cores at a tyre change, as the rubber ages.","Use the correct weight type for steel or alloy wheels, and keep paint and corrosion off the wheel seat so it sits properly on the hub.","Never mix tyres of different construction or size on one axle, and fit the best tyres on the rear axle where the manual or tyre maker advises."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Never go under a vehicle supported only by a jack. Never overtighten wheel nuts with an impact wrench, and never use oil or grease on wheel studs unless the manual says so."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"Pressure, tread depth and wear pattern together tell the story of a tyre. Read all three before you decide what to tell the customer."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m03-l7", "title": "Belts, hoses, lights and wipers", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A routine inspection covers many small items that can cause big problems if ignored. This lesson lists the checks and what you should look for."},
      {"t":"diagram","name":"belt-wear"},
      {"t":"h","text":"Drive belts"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A **drive belt** turns accessories such as the alternator, water pump, power-steering pump and air-conditioning compressor. Check for cracks, fraying, glazing, oil contamination and missing ribs. Check the tension and the condition of the tensioner and pulleys. A squeal at start-up can mean a loose or worn belt. A broken belt may stop the charging system and cooling, so replace a worn belt before it fails. A **timing belt** is separate and is replaced at the interval in the manual, because failure can wreck the engine."},
      {"t":"p","text":"Most modern engines use one **serpentine belt**, a flat belt with several ribs that drives all accessories, kept tight by an automatic tensioner. Check the tensioner arm moves freely and is in its working range, and that the pulleys spin smoothly without noise. A **timing chain** inside the engine is lubricated by the oil and lasts much longer than a belt. It can still stretch, and a rattle at start-up can warn that the chain or its guides and tensioner are worn."},
      {"t":"p","text":"When a timing belt is due, replace the whole kit: belt, tensioner, idler pulleys and often the water pump, because they work together and are costly to reach. A timing belt that is overdue can break without warning and, on an interference engine, destroy the valves and pistons."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Hoses and leaks"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Squeeze each coolant hose when the engine is cold. It should feel firm, not hard, soft or spongy. Look for swelling, cracks and wet or crusty patches at the clamps. Look for oil, coolant, fuel and brake fluid leaks under the engine and around the gearbox. A leak is a fault. Record where it is and which fluid it is."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Lights and wipers"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Check headlamps (dipped and main beam), side lights, brake lights, indicators, hazard lights, reversing lights and number-plate lights.","Check the horn and the washers.","Look at wiper blades for splits, hardening and smearing. Replace blades that do not clear the screen cleanly.","Check the windscreen for chips and cracks in the driver's view."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Battery and terminals"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Check that the battery is held down firmly and that the terminals are clean and tight. White or green powder on the terminals is corrosion, which causes poor starting and charging. Clean it and tighten the clamps. Note the age of the battery. Most car batteries last three to five years, depending on climate and use."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Brakes and steering"},
      {"t":"p","text":"With the wheels off or at least visible, check the brake pads for thickness, the discs for scoring and lips, and the flexible hoses for cracks. Check the steering for play and for torn gaiters (rubber boots) on joints and rack ends. Check suspension for leaking dampers (shock absorbers) and damaged bushes. Anything that affects safety is a red item."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Item","Typical check","Action"],"rows":[["Brake pad thickness","Measure the friction material, not including the backing plate","Plan replacement at about 3 mm. Replace at the maker's limit, often 2 mm."],["Brake discs","Check thickness, deep scoring, cracks and a lip at the edge","Replace if below the stamped minimum thickness"],["Brake hoses","Look for cracks, chafing and bulges","Replace damaged hoses"],["Steering joints","Shake each tie rod end and ball joint, check boots","Replace worn joints and split boots"],["Dampers","Look for oil wetting the body and for a bouncing ride","Replace in pairs on an axle"]],"caption":"Brake and steering faults are red items. Tell the customer in writing."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"A hand torch and an inspection mirror will show cracks and leaks that are hard to see. Use them every time."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m03-l8", "title": "Pre-delivery, pre-purchase and seasonal inspections", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"Besides the regular service, workshops carry out inspections for particular purposes. A used vehicle is checked before it is bought. A vehicle is prepared before a long trip or a change of season. A new or imported vehicle is inspected before delivery. Each has a different focus, and each depends on the same skill: look carefully, record exactly and report honestly."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Pre-purchase inspection"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A buyer asks you to check a used vehicle before they pay. Your job is to give them an honest, evidence-based report. Do not give a verdict on price, only on condition."},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Documents:** check that the VIN on the vehicle matches the papers and has not been altered, and that the ownership and service records agree.","**Odometer:** compare the reading with the wear on the pedals, seat, steering wheel and gear lever, and with the service records. Be suspicious if they do not fit.","**Body:** look along the panels for uneven gaps, ripples, different paint shades, overspray on rubbers, and new bolts on old panels. These suggest accident repair.","**Rust:** check sills, wheel arches, door bottoms, floor, chassis rails and suspension mounting points. Structural rust is a safety fault.","**Flood damage:** look for a musty smell, damp or stained carpets, silt under seats and in the boot, rust on seat rails and under the dashboard, and fog or a water line in lamps.","**Engine and fluids:** check the oil and coolant condition. Start from cold and listen. Look for smoke, leaks and warning lights that stay on.","**Scan for codes** and check the readiness status of emission systems. A recently cleared fault memory is a warning sign.","**Tyres, brakes, steering and suspension:** check as in a normal inspection and report wear.","**Road test:** drive on different surfaces and speeds, check the gearbox and clutch, braking and noises."]},
      {"t":"p","text":"Used imports need special care. Check that the odometer unit and reading are consistent with the import documents, that emission equipment such as the catalytic converter and particulate filter is still fitted, and that parts exist locally. A vehicle converted from left- to right-hand drive should be checked for the quality of the steering and pedal conversion."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Pre-delivery inspection"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A new vehicle, or one that has been in a depot for a long time, needs checking before the customer collects it. Check fluid levels, tyre pressures and wheel torque, battery charge, all lights and electrical functions, the air conditioning, the absence of transit damage and the presence of keys, manuals, a spare wheel and tools. Remove protective covers and wrappings, and reset the settings."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Before a long journey"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A pre-trip check protects the family and the load. Check the following."},
      {"t":"table","head":["Item","What to check"],"rows":[["Tyres including the spare","Pressures set for the load, tread, sidewall damage, jack and wheel brace present"],["Fluids","Oil, coolant, brake fluid, washer fluid"],["Brakes and steering","Pad thickness, pedal feel, no pulling"],["Battery","Terminals clean and tight, voltage at least 12.4 V at rest"],["Belts and hoses","Condition and tension"],["Lights and wipers","All working, blades clear the screen"],["Safety equipment","Warning triangle, first aid kit, fire extinguisher, spare bulbs and fuses, tow rope"]]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Seasonal and climate preparation"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["**Hot weather:** check the coolant level and condition, the radiator and condenser for dirt, the fan operation, the belt, the battery (heat shortens its life) and the air conditioning. Check tyre pressures cold.","**Dusty roads:** check and replace air and cabin filters more often. Grease joints that are fitted with grease nipples.","**Rainy season:** check wiper blades, washers, lights, tyre tread and brakes for the wet. Look for water leaks and blocked drains.","**Cold weather:** check antifreeze strength, battery condition, thin enough oil, and washer fluid with antifreeze.","**Storage:** for a vehicle that will stand for months, fill the tank and add stabiliser, change the oil, raise tyre pressures slightly, disconnect or maintain the battery, and keep it clean and covered."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"key","text":"An inspection report is only as good as its evidence. Write what you saw, where you saw it and how you measured it, and keep your opinions separate from your findings."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Photograph rust, leaks, worn parts and warning lights, and attach the pictures to the report. They show the customer exactly what you found."}
    ]},
    {"id": "m03-l9", "title": "Road test and handover", "minutes": 5, "body": [
      {"t":"p","text":"A road test confirms that the vehicle is safe to return to the customer. This lesson covers how to prepare, what to notice and how to hand the vehicle back."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Before the test"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Make sure the work is finished and checked: wheel nuts torqued, fluids at the correct levels, no tools left in the engine bay, seat and mirrors adjusted, and the brakes checked by pressing the pedal firmly in the workshop before moving off. You must be legally permitted to drive the vehicle on the road. Check the vehicle is insured for test driving."},
      {"t":"h","text":"During the test"},
      {"t":"ul","items":["Choose a quiet route and obey all traffic rules.","Test braking in a safe place and check that the vehicle stops in a straight line.","Listen for knocks, rattles, humming from wheel bearings, squeals from belts and whining from the transmission.","Feel for vibration through the steering wheel, pull to one side and any slipping or harsh gear changes.","Watch the gauges and warning lights, especially coolant temperature, oil pressure and charging."]},
      {"t":"note","kind":"safety","text":"Stop safely and report it if anything feels unsafe, such as a soft brake pedal, loose steering or a wheel wobble. Do not carry on driving to see if it improves."},
      {"t":"note","kind":"tip","text":"Drive the same test route each time. You will notice changes in noise and feel much more easily."},
      {"t":"table","head":["What you notice","Possible cause"],"rows":[["Pulling to one side when braking","A sticking caliper, uneven pads or a tyre problem"],["Steering wheel shakes at speed","Unbalanced front wheel, bent rim, or worn joints"],["Vehicle wanders on a straight road","Worn steering parts, wrong alignment or tyre pressures"],["Clunk over bumps","Worn bushes, drop links, top mounts or ball joints"],["Hum rising with speed","Wheel bearing or tyre noise"],["Engine hesitates or misfires","Ignition, fuel or air supply fault"],["Gear changes are harsh or slip","Low fluid, a worn clutch or a transmission fault"]],"caption":"Note the speed and conditions of each fault so you can reproduce it."},
      {"t":"h","text":"After the test"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Recheck for leaks, check that nothing is hot that should not be, and look at the fluid levels again. Fix any faults you found before handover, or record them clearly if the customer has to agree to extra work."},
      {"t":"h","text":"Handover"},
      {"t":"p","text":"Explain the work in plain words: what you did, what you found and what needs attention next. Show the customer any worn parts you replaced. Give them the job card, the next service date and the distance at which it is due. Be honest about amber items. If a repair is not urgent, say when it should be done. A clear handover builds trust and brings repeat customers."},
      {"t":"example","title":"Recording the next service","steps":["The odometer reads 84,200 km. The oil change interval is 10,000 km.","The next oil service is due at 84,200 + 10,000 = 94,200 km.","If the time interval of 12 months arrives first, the service is due by that date instead.","Write both the distance and the date on the sticker and the job card."]},
      {"t":"h","text":"Follow-up"},
      {"t":"p","text":"A good workshop checks on its work. A phone call or message a few days after a service asks whether the customer has any concerns, and shows you care. If a comeback occurs, treat it as a chance to learn: find the cause, correct it and tell the technician who did the work so it is not repeated."}
    ]}
  ],
  "practical": {"title":"Complete a basic service and inspection with a job card","objective":"Carry out a basic service on a training vehicle, inspecting items in a fixed order, changing the engine oil and filter, and completing a written job card.","time":"120 minutes","tools":["Vehicle lift or axle stands and wheel chocks","Socket set, torque wrench and oil filter wrench","Drain container, funnel and rags","Tyre pressure gauge and tread depth gauge","Correct oil, oil filter and sump plug washer for the vehicle","Workshop manual and a blank job card"],"safety":["Chock the wheels, support the vehicle on a lift or axle stands and never work under a vehicle held only by a jack.","Hot oil and exhaust parts can burn, so let the engine cool slightly before draining.","Wear gloves and safety glasses, and wipe up spilled oil immediately.","Never open the radiator cap while the engine is hot.","Put used oil and filters in the correct waste containers."],"steps":["Fill in the top of the job card with the vehicle details, odometer reading and the work requested.","Walk around the vehicle and record the condition of the bodywork, lights, wipers and windscreen.","Check and record the pressure and tread depth of each tyre, including the spare.","Open the bonnet and check the engine oil, coolant, brake fluid, power-steering and washer fluid levels.","Inspect the drive belt, hoses and battery terminals and record each item as green, amber or red.","Warm the engine briefly, raise the vehicle safely and place the drain container under the sump plug.","Drain the oil, replace the sump plug washer and tighten the plug to the manual's torque.","Remove the oil filter, check that the old seal came away, oil the new seal and fit the new filter.","Refill with the correct grade and quantity of oil and check the dipstick.","Start the engine, check for leaks and reset the service indicator if the vehicle has one.","Lower the vehicle, tighten the wheel nuts to torque if wheels were removed and clean up.","Complete the job card with the work done, parts used, findings and the next service due."],"checks":["I followed a fixed inspection order and recorded findings as green, amber or red.","I checked each fluid level correctly and used the right grade of oil.","I fitted the filter and sump plug correctly and found no leaks.","I checked tyre pressures cold and compared them with the vehicle placard.","I disposed of the used oil and filter correctly.","I completed the job card clearly with the next service date."]},
  "quiz": [
    {"id":"m03-q01","q":"What does a service schedule list?","options":["The price of each service together with the warranty terms offered to the owner","Only the parts that the workshop has in stock for that vehicle at the time","A list of every fault that the vehicle has had since it was built and sold","The tasks to do and the distance or time at which each is due"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m03-l1","explain":"A schedule tells you what to do and when, by distance or time. Always use the manufacturer's schedule for that vehicle."},
    {"id":"m03-q02","q":"Which operating condition counts as severe use?","options":["Frequent short trips in dusty, stop-start city traffic","Long steady motorway trips on well-maintained smooth roads in cool weather","A vehicle that is serviced exactly on time at every single interval","A vehicle that is parked indoors in a garage for most of the week"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m03-l1","explain":"Dust, heat, short trips and heavy loads wear fluids and filters faster, so the intervals must be shortened."},
    {"id":"m03-q03","q":"What is the purpose of a job card?","options":["To record the vehicle details, the work done, the parts used and any faults found","To list the tools that the technician used on each stage of the job","To replace the need to inspect the vehicle before work is carried out","To show the customer how much profit the workshop has made on the vehicle"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m03-l1","explain":"A job card is the written record that protects you and the customer and helps the next technician."},
    {"id":"m03-q04","q":"Why inspect a vehicle in a fixed order?","options":["So that the customer can watch each step being done in the workshop","So that no item is missed and nothing relies on memory","So that the technician does not need to write anything down on the card","So that the inspection takes the longest possible time for each vehicle"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m03-l1","explain":"A consistent order makes sure every item is checked and recorded every time."},
    {"id":"m03-q05","q":"An item on the inspection is worn but not yet unsafe. How is it rated?","options":["Green, because the item still works at the moment and has not failed","Amber, and the customer is told it will need attention soon","It is left off the job card to avoid worrying the customer about it","Red, and the vehicle is refused for any further use until replaced"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m03-l1","explain":"Amber means it is wearing and needs attention soon. Honest reporting lets the customer plan and pay for the repair."},
    {"id":"m03-q06","q":"You find a worn brake pad that was not on the job card. What should you do?","options":["Replace the pad and add the cost to the bill without telling the customer","Ignore the pad, because it was not written on the original job card","Tell the customer and agree the extra work before you carry it out","Replace it with the cheapest pad in stock and say nothing about the cost"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m03-l1","explain":"Extra work must be agreed with the customer. Silent additions damage trust and can cause disputes."},
    {"id":"m03-q07","q":"What is a severe-use vehicle?","options":["One that is driven only on motorways at a steady speed every day","One used in dust, heat, traffic or towing, so shorter intervals","One that has been fitted with new tyres within the last month","One that has a service history signed by an approved dealer"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m03-l1","explain":"Severe use wears oil, filters and brake fluid faster. Ask the customer how the vehicle is used and use the severe-use intervals."},
    {"id":"m03-q08","q":"A red oil-pressure warning light comes on while driving. What should the driver do?","options":["Stop the engine as soon as it is safe and check the oil level","Carry on to the destination, then check the oil on arrival","Top up the washer fluid and wait for the light to go off by itself","Increase engine speed to raise the oil pressure again"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m03-l1","explain":"Without oil pressure the engine can be ruined within minutes. Stop at once, and check the level and for leaks before restarting."},
    {"id":"m03-q09","q":"What does an amber engine-management light usually mean?","options":["The engine is about to stop and must be switched off now","The oil needs to be changed within the next week exactly","The fuel tank is nearly empty and must be refilled now","An engine or emission fault to check soon"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m03-l1","explain":"Amber lights signal a fault that is not an immediate danger. Read the codes soon, because some faults damage the catalytic converter."},
    {"id":"m03-q10","q":"When should the engine oil level be checked?","options":["Straight after the engine is switched off while the oil is still very hot","With the vehicle parked on a steep slope so the oil collects at one end","With the engine running at high speed so the oil level shows clearly","With the vehicle on level ground and the engine off for a few minutes"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m03-l2","explain":"On level ground with the engine off for a few minutes lets the oil drain back to the sump for a true reading."},
    {"id":"m03-q11","q":"What does 5W-30 mean in the oil grade?","options":["The 5W shows how well the oil flows cold and the 30 shows its thickness when hot","The oil must be changed every 5 months or after 30 weeks of use","The oil contains 5% water and 30% additive by volume in the can","The oil suits engines of 5 cylinders and an output of 30 kW only"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m03-l2","explain":"The number before the W is the cold flow rating, and the second number is the viscosity at running temperature."},
    {"id":"m03-q12","q":"Why must you never open a radiator cap on a hot engine?","options":["The system is under pressure and the hot coolant can scald you","The coolant will damage the paint on the bonnet if it splashes on it","The cap will not fit back on properly once it has been removed hot","The engine will stop if the cap is taken off while it is still hot"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m03-l2","explain":"The cooling system works under pressure. Opening a cap on a hot engine can release scalding coolant."},
    {"id":"m03-q13","q":"The brake fluid level in the reservoir is low. What could this mean?","options":["The brakes are cold and the fluid has shrunk back into the reservoir","The fluid has been used up during normal braking and must be refilled","The ABS system has built up too much pressure in the lines overnight","Worn brake pads, or a leak in the system that needs finding"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m03-l2","explain":"Fluid moves down as pads wear, but a falling level can also mean a leak. Both need a check."},
    {"id":"m03-q14","q":"Why should brake fluid be renewed at set intervals?","options":["It absorbs water over time, which lowers its boiling point and causes corrosion","It becomes too thick to flow once it is more than a year old","It turns to oil after one year and stops working as a brake fluid","It evaporates quickly and leaves the brake lines completely empty"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m03-l2","explain":"Water in the fluid can boil under heavy braking, causing a spongy pedal, and it also corrodes parts."},
    {"id":"m03-q15","q":"The oil on the dipstick looks milky. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The engine has been running too cold on a normal day in the workshop","The oil is brand new and of exactly the correct grade for the engine","The oil has been filtered too well by the new oil filter that was fitted","Coolant may be leaking into the oil"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m03-l2","explain":"Milky oil suggests coolant contamination, for example from a failed head gasket. It needs further tests."},
    {"id":"m03-q16","q":"What does milky oil on the dipstick usually indicate?","options":["The oil is brand new and ready to use again","The oil has the correct viscosity grade installed","Coolant or moisture mixing with the oil","The engine has been running at a very low speed"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m03-l2","explain":"Milky oil can mean a head gasket leak or a crack that lets coolant in, or condensation from short trips. Find the cause before you refill."},
    {"id":"m03-q17","q":"A brown, oily drip appears under the front of an engine. Which fluid is it most likely to be?","options":["Brake fluid","Air conditioning condensation","Petrol","Engine oil"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m03-l2","explain":"Dark brown or black oily drips are typically engine oil, from the sump, drain plug, rocker cover or seals."},
    {"id":"m03-q18","q":"The brake fluid level is steadily falling but there is no visible leak. What is the most likely reason?","options":["The brake fluid is evaporating slowly through the cap","The brake booster is using the fluid up as fuel","The pads are wearing, so the calipers hold more fluid","The fluid is changing into a gas inside the pipes"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m03-l2","explain":"As pads wear, the caliper pistons move out and more fluid sits in the calipers, so the reservoir level drops. Check the pad thickness. A sudden drop suggests a leak."},
    {"id":"m03-q19","q":"What does the W in 5W-30 stand for?","options":["Winter, which describes how the oil flows when cold","Weight, which describes the mass of the oil in the sump","Water, which describes how much moisture it can hold","Wear, which describes how much protection it gives"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m03-l3","explain":"The number before the W shows cold-flow performance. A lower number flows more easily in the cold. The second number describes hot viscosity."},
    {"id":"m03-q20","q":"Why should coolant be mixed with distilled water?","options":["Distilled water freezes at a much lower temperature","Tap water minerals leave scale and corrosion","Tap water makes the coolant change colour completely","Distilled water is cheaper than ordinary tap water"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m03-l3","explain":"Hard water leaves deposits and promotes corrosion. Deionised or distilled water keeps the system clean."},
    {"id":"m03-q21","q":"Why is DOT 5 brake fluid never mixed with DOT 4?","options":["DOT 5 has a lower boiling point than DOT 4 fluid","DOT 4 is only used in vehicles with drum brakes","It is silicone-based and does not mix with glycol","Mixing them makes the fluid turn solid in the pipes"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m03-l3","explain":"Silicone and glycol fluids do not blend. Mixing them causes poor performance and seal damage. Use the grade the manual specifies."},
    {"id":"m03-q22","q":"Why does brake fluid need to be replaced regularly?","options":["It absorbs water, which lowers its boiling point","It turns to oil with time, which makes the pedal feel hard","It evaporates from the reservoir cap, so the level drops","It loses its colour, which prevents it from lubricating"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m03-l3","explain":"Glycol brake fluid absorbs moisture. Wet fluid boils at a lower temperature, so a hard stop can make vapour bubbles and cause pedal fade."},
    {"id":"m03-q23","q":"Which gear oil class can attack brass synchroniser rings in some gearboxes?","options":["GL-4, because it contains no extreme-pressure additives at all","GL-5, because of its strong extreme-pressure additives","ATF, because it is too thin to protect the gears","Engine oil, because it is too thick to circulate"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m03-l3","explain":"GL-5 additives can corrode brass in some manual gearboxes. Use the class the manual specifies."},
    {"id":"m03-q24","q":"What should you check before fitting a new oil filter?","options":["That the new filter has a different colour from the old one on the engine","That the engine is running at high speed while the filter is fitted","That the old filter's rubber seal came off with the old filter","That the sump plug has been fitted loosely so oil can still drain out"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m03-l4","explain":"A second seal stuck on the housing would let oil escape at high pressure and empty the engine."},
    {"id":"m03-q25","q":"Why is a new sump plug washer fitted at each oil change?","options":["A washer is only needed at the very first oil change of the vehicle","A fresh washer seals reliably and does not need excess torque","The old washer always contains metal that harms the oil in the engine","The washer is part of the oil quantity measurement on the dipstick"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m03-l4","explain":"Compressed washers lose their seal. Replacing them avoids leaks without over-tightening the plug."},
    {"id":"m03-q26","q":"What can happen if the sump plug is over-tightened?","options":["The oil will flow through the filter too slowly after the plug is fitted","The thread in the sump can be stripped and cause a leak","The engine will run at a higher temperature than it did before the change","The oil pressure gauge will read a higher number at idle afterwards"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m03-l4","explain":"Stripped sump threads are costly to repair. Tighten to the torque in the workshop manual."},
    {"id":"m03-q27","q":"The dipstick shows oil above the maximum mark after a service. Why is this a problem?","options":["The excess oil can foam and reduce lubrication, and may damage the engine","It will make the oil filter work harder and wear out faster than usual","It is not a problem because the engine uses up the extra oil quickly","It will make the engine run cooler and last longer than it would normally"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m03-l4","explain":"Overfilling lets the crankshaft whip the oil into foam, which lubricates poorly and can raise crankcase pressure."},
    {"id":"m03-q28","q":"What should you do with used engine oil?","options":["Burn it in an open drum outside the workshop at the end of the day","Take it to an approved collection point in a labelled container","Pour it onto bare ground away from the buildings so it soaks in","Mix it with water and pour the mixture carefully down a floor drain"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m03-l4","explain":"Used oil is hazardous waste. It must go to an approved collector and never into the soil or water."},
    {"id":"m03-q29","q":"A blocked air filter has what effect on the engine?","options":["It stops the oil from circulating through the engine's main gallery","It increases power and reduces fuel use because less dust reaches the engine","It reduces power and increases fuel use","It cools the engine more than normal by drawing extra air into it"],"answer":2,"level":1,"lesson":"m03-l4","explain":"A restricted air supply upsets the mixture and the engine breathes poorly."},
    {"id":"m03-q30","q":"Why must the old oil filter's rubber seal be checked after removal?","options":["The seal can be reused on the new filter without any risk","The seal shows the date that the engine was built","The seal tells you the exact grade of oil to use","A second seal left on the housing causes a major leak"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m03-l4","explain":"If the old seal sticks to the housing and the new filter is fitted on top, the two seals leak badly under pressure. Check that the old one came off."},
    {"id":"m03-q31","q":"After refilling the engine with oil, how should you check the level?","options":["Run it briefly, stop, wait, then read the dipstick","Read the dipstick immediately while the engine is running","Fill until oil overflows from the filler neck","Assume the level is right if you add the stated amount"],"answer":0,"level":2,"lesson":"m03-l4","explain":"The filter and galleries take some oil when the engine runs. Stop, wait for the oil to drain back, and then read the dipstick. Never overfill."},
    {"id":"m03-q32","q":"A diesel engine has a particulate filter. Why might the manual call for low-ash oil?","options":["Low-ash oil is thinner and so makes the engine run quieter","Ash from ordinary oil can block the particulate filter","Ordinary oil does not lubricate diesel engines at all","Low-ash oil prevents the filter from getting hot"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m03-l4","explain":"Ash from burnt oil additives collects in the particulate filter and cannot be burnt off. Low-ash oil prevents early blockage."},
    {"id":"m03-q33","q":"How can you tell that a paper air filter is blocked?","options":["It feels warm to the touch after the engine has been run","It smells of oil when you remove it from the filter box","It has a different colour at each end of the element","You cannot see light through it when held up"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m03-l5","explain":"A filter that blocks the light will also block the air. Replace it. Do not wash or oil a paper element."},
    {"id":"m03-q34","q":"Why should the fill plug of a gearbox be opened before the drain plug?","options":["To release the pressure in the gearbox before it is drained","To warm the gearbox oil so that it drains more quickly","So you know you can refill it before draining","To check the colour of the oil before you open the drain"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m03-l5","explain":"If the fill plug is seized and cannot be opened, you would have drained the gearbox with no way to refill it. Always open the fill plug first."},
    {"id":"m03-q35","q":"What is the correct first step before changing spark plugs?","options":["Run the engine at high speed to burn the deposits off","Spray water around the plugs to cool the cylinder head","Disconnect the brake pipes to prevent a build-up of pressure","Let the engine cool and clean around the plugs"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m03-l5","explain":"Removing plugs from a hot aluminium head can damage the thread. Dirt around the plug can fall into the cylinder when it is removed."},
    {"id":"m03-q36","q":"After refilling a cooling system, why is it important to bleed the air out?","options":["Air in the system makes the coolant change colour","Air raises the boiling point and so wastes fuel","Trapped air causes hot spots and overheating","Air helps the coolant to flow faster round the engine"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m03-l5","explain":"Air pockets do not carry heat away, so parts near them run hot and the engine can overheat. Bleed using the screws and the heater, and recheck the level when cold."},
    {"id":"m03-q37","q":"During a brake fluid change, which rule helps to prevent air entering the system?","options":["Always open all four bleed screws at the same time","Never let the master cylinder reservoir run dry","Only change the fluid when the engine is running","Keep the brake pedal pressed to the floor while moving between wheels"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m03-l5","explain":"If the reservoir empties, air enters the master cylinder and a much longer bleed is needed. Keep topping up as fluid leaves the system."},
    {"id":"m03-q38","q":"What does 205 mean in a tyre marked 205/55 R16?","options":["The tyre width in millimetres","The speed rating of the tyre in kilometres per hour","The maximum load of the tyre in kilograms per wheel","The rim diameter of the wheel in millimetres across"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m03-l6","explain":"The first number is the section width in mm. The 55 is the aspect ratio and 16 is the rim diameter in inches."},
    {"id":"m03-q39","q":"What does the 55 mean in a tyre marked 205/55 R16?","options":["The tyre was made in the year 2055 at the factory","The tyre must be inflated to 55 psi when it is fitted","The sidewall height is 55% of the tyre width","The tread depth is 5.5 mm when the tyre is brand new"],"answer":2,"level":3,"lesson":"m03-l6","explain":"The aspect ratio shows sidewall height as a percentage of the width."},
    {"id":"m03-q40","q":"Both edges of a tyre are worn more than the centre. What is the likely cause?","options":["Over-inflation, which makes the middle of the tread carry the load","A worn wheel bearing on one side of the vehicle only","Under-inflation","Incorrect tyre rotation at the last service and nothing else"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m03-l6","explain":"A soft tyre sags and the edges carry more load. Over-inflation wears the centre."},
    {"id":"m03-q41","q":"When should tyre pressures be checked?","options":["When the tyres are cold, using the figure on the vehicle placard","Using the maximum pressure that is moulded onto the tyre sidewall","Straight after a long fast drive when the tyres are still very hot","Only when a tyre warning light shows on the dashboard of the car"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m03-l6","explain":"Driving heats the air and raises the pressure. The sidewall figure is a limit, not the recommended pressure."},
    {"id":"m03-q42","q":"A tyre has a bulge in the sidewall. What should you do?","options":["Add more pressure to the tyre so that the bulge is pushed back in","Replace the tyre, because the bulge shows internal damage","Repair it with a patch from the inside of the tyre and refit it","Rotate the tyre to the rear axle and carry on using it as normal"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m03-l6","explain":"A sidewall bulge means the cords have broken. The tyre can burst and cannot be repaired."},
    {"id":"m03-q43","q":"How should wheel nuts be tightened after refitting a wheel?","options":["Only one nut at a time, fully tight, before the next one is started","In a crisscross order to the specified torque, and rechecked after about 50 km","As tight as the technician can manage with a long bar on the wrench","In a clockwise circle with an impact wrench at full power on each nut"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m03-l6","explain":"A crisscross order seats the wheel evenly, and a torque wrench prevents distortion or loose nuts."},
    {"id":"m03-q44","q":"A tyre has the DOT code 3523 at its end. What does it tell you?","options":["It was made in week 35 of 2023","It can carry a load of 3,523 kg","It should be inflated to 35 psi in 2023","It has been retreaded 35 times"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m03-l6","explain":"The four digits of the DOT code are the week and the last two digits of the year of manufacture. Tyres age even when they are not used."},
    {"id":"m03-q45","q":"Why is it important to set tyre pressure when the tyres are cold?","options":["Cold tyres have much thinner sidewalls to measure","The gauge only works at low temperatures","Driving heats the air and raises the reading","The valve will not open on a warm tyre"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m03-l6","explain":"A warm tyre can read 0.2 to 0.4 bar higher than the cold pressure. Setting a warm tyre to the cold value would leave it under-inflated."},
    {"id":"m03-q46","q":"A tyre is worn on both edges but not in the centre. What is the most likely cause?","options":["It is under-inflated, so the edges carry the load","It is over-inflated, so the edges carry the load","The wheel alignment toe angle is set very wide","The wheel is out of balance and bounces on the road"],"answer":0,"level":3,"lesson":"m03-l6","explain":"Under-inflation lets the centre of the tread lift and the edges wear. Over-inflation wears the centre."},
    {"id":"m03-q47","q":"What should you look for when inspecting a drive belt?","options":["Whether the belt is warm to the touch when the engine is cold","Whether the belt has some dust on the outside surface of the ribs","Cracks, fraying, glazing, oil contamination and missing ribs","Only the colour of the belt and how clean the outside looks"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m03-l7","explain":"These signs show wear or contamination that can cause slipping or failure."},
    {"id":"m03-q48","q":"A coolant hose feels soft and swollen when squeezed cold. What does this suggest?","options":["The coolant is too cold and should be warmed up before use","The hose is in perfect condition and should be left as it is","The clamp has been over-tightened by the factory on assembly","The hose is deteriorating inside and should be replaced"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m03-l7","explain":"A soft or swollen hose has weakened and can burst when hot and under pressure."},
    {"id":"m03-q49","q":"What does white or green powder around a battery terminal indicate?","options":["That acid has been added to the battery by mistake at some point","That the battery is too new to use and should be left in the box","A fully charged battery in good health that is ready for use","Corrosion, which can cause poor starting and charging"],"answer":3,"level":1,"lesson":"m03-l7","explain":"Corrosion adds resistance. Clean it off and tighten the clamp."},
    {"id":"m03-q50","q":"Which item on a lighting check must be tested, and why?","options":["The interior lamp only, because the passengers use it the most","The dashboard lights only, because the driver is the one who sees them","Only the headlights, because they are the brightest lights on the car","Brake lights, because a failed one stops following drivers from seeing you brake"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m03-l7","explain":"Every exterior lamp has a safety purpose. A failed brake light is a common cause of rear-end collisions."},
    {"id":"m03-q51","q":"Why is a timing belt, tensioner and water pump often replaced together?","options":["They are always made by the same company at the same plant","They work together, and the labour to reach them is largely the same","The water pump keeps the belt cool and so needs the same part number","It is required by law in every country that has roads"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m03-l7","explain":"If one part of the drive fails soon after the belt is renewed, the same labour is needed again. Replacing the kit avoids this and prevents belt failure."},
    {"id":"m03-q52","q":"A brake pad is measured at 2.5 mm of friction material. What should you do?","options":["Leave it, because it will last for another year","Replace only the inner pad and leave the outer pad in place","Fit a thicker backing plate to make up the difference","Report it as near the limit and plan replacement"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m03-l7","explain":"Pads are usually replaced at about 2 to 3 mm. Report it as a red or amber item. Always replace pads in axle sets."},
    {"id":"m03-q53","q":"What does a squeal at start-up usually suggest?","options":["A loose or worn drive belt","A flat spare tyre in the boot","Low windscreen washer fluid","A discharged key fob battery"],"answer":0,"level":1,"lesson":"m03-l7","explain":"A slipping belt squeals, often worse when wet or when the engine is cold. Check its condition, tension and the tensioner."},
    {"id":"m03-q54","q":"During a pre-purchase inspection, which finding suggests a car has been in an accident?","options":["A clean engine bay and a freshly valeted interior","Uneven gaps and overspray on the seals","A full set of matching tyres of the same make","A service stamp for every year of the vehicle's life"],"answer":1,"level":2,"lesson":"m03-l8","explain":"Uneven gaps and overspray show that panels were removed or repainted. They can be a sign of major accident repair, which could affect safety."},
    {"id":"m03-q55","q":"Which of these is a sign of possible flood damage in a used vehicle?","options":["A strong smell of new leather in the cabin","New floor mats and tinted windows","A full tank of fuel and a clean boot","Silt under seats and rust on seat rails"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m03-l8","explain":"Flood water leaves mud, rust in unusual places and a musty smell. It also damages electronics, which may fail months later."},
    {"id":"m03-q56","q":"Why is a recently cleared fault memory a warning sign during a pre-purchase check?","options":["Clearing codes always makes the engine run better","It shows that the car has been serviced by a dealer","The seller may have cleared codes to hide a fault","It means the warning lights were never connected"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m03-l8","explain":"Clearing codes erases fault history. Readiness monitors for emission systems stay incomplete for a while afterwards, which shows that the codes were cleared recently."},
    {"id":"m03-q57","q":"What must you check before taking a vehicle on a road test?","options":["That the radio and the clock have been set to the right time","That the customer is sitting in the passenger seat and has agreed","That the work is finished, wheel nuts are torqued and the brakes work when pressed in the workshop","That the fuel tank has been filled to the very top of the filler"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m03-l9","explain":"A road test follows completed, checked work. Check brakes before moving off."},
    {"id":"m03-q58","q":"During a road test the brake pedal feels soft and travels too far. What should you do?","options":["Pump it several times and continue with the test as planned","Return to the workshop at speed so you can work on the pedal","Ignore it, because new brakes always feel soft and need time to bed in","Stop safely, report it and do not carry on driving the vehicle"],"answer":3,"level":3,"lesson":"m03-l9","explain":"A soft pedal can mean air or a leak in the brakes. Do not continue the test."},
    {"id":"m03-q59","q":"A vehicle has an odometer reading of 62,500 km and the oil interval is 10,000 km. When is the next oil change due?","options":["At 62,510 km, ten kilometres after the current reading","At 725,000 km, ten times the current odometer reading","At 72,500 km","At 52,500 km, which was ten thousand kilometres ago"],"answer":2,"level":2,"lesson":"m03-l9","explain":"Add the interval to the current reading: 62,500 + 10,000 = 72,500 km. A time limit may arrive first."},
    {"id":"m03-q60","q":"How should you explain an amber item to a customer?","options":["Say nothing, because the item is not yet urgent for the customer","Say what it is, why it will need attention and roughly when","Say that it will fail very soon and must be replaced immediately","Tell them it is not a problem because it still works at the moment"],"answer":1,"level":3,"lesson":"m03-l9","explain":"Honest, clear information lets the customer plan the repair and builds trust."},
    {"id":"m03-q61","q":"What must be checked before you drive a vehicle out of the workshop on a road test?","options":["The radio is tuned to the customer's favourite station","Wheel nuts are torqued and the brake pedal is firm","The service sticker has been placed on the windscreen","The tyres have been cleaned with tyre shine"],"answer":1,"level":1,"lesson":"m03-l9","explain":"Before moving off, check that nothing is loose and that the brake pedal is firm. A road test can only be done safely on a vehicle that is safe to drive."},
    {"id":"m03-q62","q":"The odometer reads 84,200 km and the oil change interval is 10,000 km. When is the next change due?","options":["At 84,210 km, ten kilometres after the last service","At 104,200 km, which adds the interval twice","At 74,200 km, which subtracts the interval","At 94,200 km, or by the date if that is sooner"],"answer":3,"level":2,"lesson":"m03-l9","explain":"Add the interval to the current reading: 84,200 + 10,000 = 94,200 km. If the time interval arrives first, the service is due by that date."}
  ],
  "cards": [
    {"id":"m03-c01","front":"What is a job card?","back":"A written record of the vehicle, the work requested, the faults found, the parts used and the work done.","lesson":"m03-l1"},
    {"id":"m03-c02","front":"What is severe use?","back":"Operating conditions that wear a vehicle faster, such as dust, heat, short trips, towing and heavy loads.","lesson":"m03-l1"},
    {"id":"m03-c03","front":"What does a green, amber and red rating mean?","back":"Green: good. Amber: wearing and needs attention soon. Red: unsafe or failed and needs attention now.","lesson":"m03-l1"},
    {"id":"m03-c04","front":"Why inspect in a fixed order?","back":"So that no item is missed and findings are recorded as you go.","lesson":"m03-l1"},
    {"id":"m03-c05","front":"Name the three levels of inspection findings.","back":"Green: good. Amber: will need attention soon. Red: unsafe or failed, needs attention now.","lesson":"m03-l1"},
    {"id":"m03-c06","front":"What does a red oil pressure light mean?","back":"Low oil pressure. Stop the engine at once and check the level.","lesson":"m03-l1"},
    {"id":"m03-c07","front":"When is the engine oil level checked?","back":"On level ground with the engine off for a few minutes, between the minimum and maximum marks.","lesson":"m03-l2"},
    {"id":"m03-c08","front":"What does the W in 5W-30 stand for?","back":"Winter. The number before it shows how well the oil flows when cold.","lesson":"m03-l2"},
    {"id":"m03-c09","front":"When may you check coolant level?","back":"Only when the engine is cold. Never open the radiator cap on a hot engine.","lesson":"m03-l2"},
    {"id":"m03-c10","front":"Why renew brake fluid at intervals?","back":"It absorbs water, which lowers its boiling point and causes corrosion.","lesson":"m03-l2"},
    {"id":"m03-c11","front":"What does milky engine oil suggest?","back":"Coolant may be mixing with the oil.","lesson":"m03-l2"},
    {"id":"m03-c12","front":"What does a milky oil on the dipstick suggest?","back":"Coolant or moisture in the oil, such as a head gasket leak, a cracked head or condensation.","lesson":"m03-l2"},
    {"id":"m03-c13","front":"What does 5W-30 mean?","back":"The 5W number shows cold flow (lower flows more easily). The 30 shows thickness at running temperature.","lesson":"m03-l3"},
    {"id":"m03-c14","front":"What do API and ACEA do?","back":"They set performance specifications for engine oil. API S categories are for petrol, C for diesel. ACEA C classes are low-ash oils for filters.","lesson":"m03-l3"},
    {"id":"m03-c15","front":"Why must coolant types not be mixed without checking?","back":"The corrosion additives differ (IAT, OAT, HOAT). Mixing can cause gelling and poor protection. Colour is not a safe guide.","lesson":"m03-l3"},
    {"id":"m03-c16","front":"Dry boiling points of DOT 3, 4 and 5.1?","back":"At least 205 °C, 230 °C and 260 °C. Wet values are much lower: 140 °C, 155 °C and 180 °C.","lesson":"m03-l3"},
    {"id":"m03-c17","front":"What is the difference between GL-4 and GL-5 gear oil?","back":"GL-5 has stronger extreme-pressure additives and can corrode brass synchroniser rings in some gearboxes. Use the class the manual states.","lesson":"m03-l3"},
    {"id":"m03-c18","front":"Why fit a new sump plug washer?","back":"A fresh washer seals the plug without extra torque.","lesson":"m03-l4"},
    {"id":"m03-c19","front":"What must you check when fitting a new oil filter?","back":"That the old filter's seal came off with it. A double seal causes a major leak.","lesson":"m03-l4"},
    {"id":"m03-c20","front":"What happens if you overfill the engine oil?","back":"The oil can foam and lubricate poorly, which may damage the engine.","lesson":"m03-l4"},
    {"id":"m03-c21","front":"What does an air filter protect against?","back":"Dust and dirt entering the engine.","lesson":"m03-l4"},
    {"id":"m03-c22","front":"How should used oil be disposed of?","back":"In a labelled container taken to an approved collection point.","lesson":"m03-l4"},
    {"id":"m03-c23","front":"What are the two types of oil filter?","back":"Spin-on (a sealed canister, replaced whole) and cartridge (a paper element in a reusable housing with a seal).","lesson":"m03-l4"},
    {"id":"m03-c24","front":"How do you check a paper air filter?","back":"Hold it to a light. If you cannot see light through it, replace it. Never wash or oil a paper element.","lesson":"m03-l5"},
    {"id":"m03-c25","front":"What colour of spark plug indicates a rich mixture?","back":"Black and sooty. White or blistered suggests overheating. Light grey-brown is normal.","lesson":"m03-l5"},
    {"id":"m03-c26","front":"Order of plugs when changing gearbox oil?","back":"Open the fill plug first, then the drain plug. Refill until oil runs from the fill hole.","lesson":"m03-l5"},
    {"id":"m03-c27","front":"Where do you start when bleeding brakes?","back":"At the wheel furthest from the master cylinder, unless the manual says otherwise. Never let the reservoir run dry.","lesson":"m03-l5"},
    {"id":"m03-c28","front":"What does 205/55 R16 mean?","back":"205 mm wide, sidewall 55% of the width, radial construction, 16 inch rim.","lesson":"m03-l6"},
    {"id":"m03-c29","front":"What do both edges of a tyre worn mean?","back":"Under-inflation.","lesson":"m03-l6"},
    {"id":"m03-c30","front":"What does centre tread wear mean?","back":"Over-inflation.","lesson":"m03-l6"},
    {"id":"m03-c31","front":"When should tyre pressures be checked?","back":"When the tyres are cold, using the pressure on the vehicle placard.","lesson":"m03-l6"},
    {"id":"m03-c32","front":"What does a bulge in a tyre sidewall mean?","back":"Internal damage. The tyre must be replaced.","lesson":"m03-l6"},
    {"id":"m03-c33","front":"How should wheel nuts be tightened?","back":"In a crisscross order to the specified torque, and rechecked after about 50 km.","lesson":"m03-l6"},
    {"id":"m03-c34","front":"What does a DOT date code on a tyre show?","back":"The week and year of manufacture. 3523 means week 35 of 2023.","lesson":"m03-l6"},
    {"id":"m03-c35","front":"Why does tyre pressure fall in cold weather?","back":"The air contracts. Pressure drops about 0.1 bar for every 10 °C fall in temperature.","lesson":"m03-l6"},
    {"id":"m03-c36","front":"What does a squealing belt at start-up suggest?","back":"A loose or worn drive belt.","lesson":"m03-l7"},
    {"id":"m03-c37","front":"How should a coolant hose feel when squeezed cold?","back":"Firm. Soft, spongy or swollen hoses should be replaced.","lesson":"m03-l7"},
    {"id":"m03-c38","front":"What is white powder on a battery terminal?","back":"Corrosion. Clean it and tighten the clamp.","lesson":"m03-l7"},
    {"id":"m03-c39","front":"What should be renewed with a timing belt?","back":"The tensioner, the idler pulleys and often the water pump, because they work together and are costly to reach.","lesson":"m03-l7"},
    {"id":"m03-c40","front":"Signs of flood damage in a used car?","back":"Musty smell, damp carpets, silt under seats, rust on seat rails and under the dash, water lines in lamps.","lesson":"m03-l8"},
    {"id":"m03-c41","front":"Signs of accident repair?","back":"Uneven panel gaps, paint overspray on seals, different shades of paint and new bolts on old panels.","lesson":"m03-l8"},
    {"id":"m03-c42","front":"What should you check before a road test?","back":"That the work is finished, wheel nuts are torqued and the brakes work when pressed.","lesson":"m03-l9"},
    {"id":"m03-c43","front":"What should you do if the brakes feel unsafe on a road test?","back":"Stop safely, report it and do not continue driving.","lesson":"m03-l9"},
    {"id":"m03-c44","front":"What should the handover include?","back":"What was done, what was found, items to watch, the job card and the next service date.","lesson":"m03-l9"}
  ]
});
